Body:
This is an interesting idea. We’re going to play with it and see how well the agent actually does when the orchestrator can dispatch into service-local Locations.

A useful experiment would be a custom plugin that shadows the built-in `subagent` tool and makes `directory` explicit. Roughly:

```ts
// .opencode/plugins/subagent-at.ts
import { Plugin } from "@opencode-ai/plugin/v2"

export default Plugin.define({
  id: "experiment.subagent-at",
  setup: async (ctx) => {
    await ctx.tool.transform((tools) => {
      // A later registration with the same name shadows the built-in tool.
      tools.add({
        name: "subagent",
        description: "Run a subagent in an explicit project directory",
        jsonSchema: {
          type: "object",
          properties: {
            agent: { type: "string" },
            description: { type: "string" },
            prompt: { type: "string" },
            directory: { type: "string" },
          },
          required: ["agent", "description", "prompt", "directory"],
          additionalProperties: false,
        },
        execute: async (raw, tool) => {
          const input = raw as {
            agent: string
            description: string
            prompt: string
            directory: string
          }

          const child = await ctx.session.create({
            agent: input.agent,
            location: { directory: input.directory },
            // This is the key API addition needed for a true replacement:
            parentID: tool.sessionID,
          })

          await ctx.session.prompt({
            sessionID: child.id,
            text: input.prompt,
          })

          return {
            structured: { sessionID: child.id, directory: input.directory },
            content: [
              {
                type: "text",
                text: `Started ${input.description} in ${input.directory} (${child.id})`,
              },
            ],
          }
        },
      })
    })
  },
})
```

Today, omitting `parentID` should let us test the directory-local agent/config/instruction behavior, but it creates an unlinked session. Exposing `parentID` on `ctx.session.create`—plus wait/background/cancellation and cross-Location event visibility—would let this become a faithful plugin replacement rather than only a partial experiment.
